i thought in the beginning it might be a  kind of contemporary upstairs downstairs  story  and then jackie was very open to the  idea um so she she was a mod she had  been a model she's very comfortable in  front of the camera both she and david  were very proud of their project of  building the biggest house in america  david was very proud of his legacy  and so we started um as a portrait of  this family with the backdrop of  building the biggest house in america we  were a small crew we were four people  which coming from photography actually  feels big but the amazing thing about  filming there is we were working in a 26  000 square foot house so we could be  four people and still be a fly on the  wall so that was kind of amazing and i  was definitely reminded of that when i  went to binghamton new york jackie's  hometown and all of a sudden we were in  small houses and i felt like a bull in a  china shop and i would sometimes ask my  crew to wait outside when nothing was  happening so we won't overwhelm the  household but when we were in jackie's  house we really became a part of the a  part of the fabric of the house there we  could be four people we got to know all  the different members of the household  including the  nannies and the domestic help and the  other thing that was unusual about their  life is they were used to non-family  members going through all the time so  they didn't act differently in front of  guests they were used to staff and  gardeners and  extended the kids too obviously and the  kids too and so we became part of that  entourage we filmed ten times over a  three year period  and usually between five and ten days  and very intense days i mean we were  practically living with the family and  the thing about jackie is she moves very  fast  she's very unpredictable and so we were  always kind of running to keep up with  her every time  we went something completely unexpected  happened and something that really  made the story progress in a way we  couldn't have imagined i mean it all it  always felt like there was never a  wasted day  because something incredible would  happen and  um but usually very unexpected i mean we  actually just went for a mundane family  birthday party in the middle of 2010 and  then happened to be there when the house  went on the market and that was really  the turn in the story and then i did an  interview with david where he revealed  that he had never taken anything off the  table and had personally signed for all  the loans and so it was only then that i  realized what the stakes were because  before that even after the crash i never  thought these were people who had any  vulnerability to the vicissitudes of the  market because they had so much  insulation well i took the film to the  sundance lab last summer and that really  transformed the film i worked with joe  bieni who's edited a lot of verner  herzog's films and  he helped me kind of craft a new  beginning and actually tempt in wagner  music for the beginning and it gave the  film an epic feel we talked about kind  of references to shakespeare and citizen  kane and  the film got bigger in a way and  and and i thought when i came out of the  lab that it shouldn't have a kind of  documentary score but it should really  have a big score an orchestral score  which  doing a documentary we didn't have  anywhere near the money for but jeff  beale really fell in love with the  project and  had a great story himself because he  actually  lived in um a house that he bought out  of foreclosure a mcmansion that he  bought because he could have a full  orchestra play in his living room and  actually we started filming in early  2009  and at that time they were the people  that would never be affected so i  thought i was doing an inside view of  wealth and looking at the american dream  and how the house had gotten bigger and  bigger  and  also looking at a real cinema verite  documentation of a wealthy family  because most of what we see of wealth is  not cinema verite but it's very  constructed whether it's reality tv or  fictional and so to kind of get the  inside look and when  they had to put their house up for sale  by that point i had been photographing  the bust in a lot of different ways i  had photographed foreclosure cities in  the inland empire i'd photographed the  crash in dubai so when they put their  house had to put up their dream house  for sale it was very familiar and i  realized at that point that this was  really an allegory about the  overreaching of america and kind of an  epic super-sized version of what we had  all gone through and that really changed  my approach to the film but it also the  the whole tone of what was going on  changed too because in the house for the  first time there wasn't kind of  both boasting excitement but there was a  lot of stress and strain and dysfunction  and  and um tension in the relationships and  and i realized at a certain point that  it was david and jackie's love story and  in a way the house was kind of their  love child and looking at how this  that part of the dream fell apart too
 

